<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alaungpaya</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1714">1714</a>–<a href="/years/1760">1760</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Aung Zeya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alaungpaya was the founder of the Konbaung dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>, reigning from 1752 to 1760. Born Aung Zeya, a headman of the village of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shwebo">Moksobo</a>, he resisted the Restored Hanthawaddy kingdom after its capture of Ava, made Moksobo his capital as Shwebo and reunified Burma within a few years. He founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangon">Rangoon</a> in 1755 and died during a campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> in Siam.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alaungpaya.jpg" alt="Alaungpaya" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Phyo WP · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alaungpaya.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Alaungpaya was born Aung Zeya on 24 August 1714 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shwebo">Moksobo</a>, a village of a few hundred households in the Mu River Valley about 100 km north-west of Ava. He was the second son of a family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentry">gentry</a> that had administered the valley for generations. He grew up while the authority of the Taungoo dynasty declined and Manipuri raiders ransacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-burma">Upper Burma</a>. He took over his father's duties as village chief in his early twenties and in 1736 was given a title at Ava and made deputy to his uncle, the lord of the valley.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1740 the Mon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-burma">Lower Burma</a> founded the Restored Hanthawaddy Kingdom at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bago">Bago</a>. By early 1752 its forces, aided by firearms supplied by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-east-india-company">French East India Company</a> and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and Portuguese mercenaries, had reached the gates of Ava. Aung Zeya refused to submit, persuaded 46 villages of the Mu Valley to join him, and on 29 February 1752 proclaimed himself king as Alaungpaya, founding the Konbaung dynasty. Ava fell to Hanthawaddy on 23 March 1752, and he stockaded his village, now renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shwebo">Shwebo</a>. His forces retook Ava on 3 January 1754.</p><p>In January 1755 he invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-burma">Lower Burma</a>. By May his armies held the Irrawaddy Delta and he captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagan">Dagon</a>, which he renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangon">Yangon</a>. The French-defended port of Thanlyin repelled several attacks and fell only after a siege of 14 months, in July 1756. Konbaung forces sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bago">Bago</a> in May 1757, ending the Hanthawaddy kingdom. In 1758 he sent an expedition to the northern Shan states, and on 6 October 1759 a Konbaung force overran the fort of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> at Cape Negrais.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Mon rebels found shelter in Siam, and in December 1759 Alaungpaya left Martaban with an army of 40,000 to invade it. The army reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> in April 1760, but five days into the siege he fell ill. Thai sources say he was wounded when a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> exploded, while Burmese sources say he fell ill. The Burmese began their retreat on 17 April 1760, and he died on 11 May 1760 at Kinywa, near Martaban. His eldest son Naungdawgyi succeeded him. The Burmese historian Htin Aung describes him as leaving a people united and confident, though denied the time to calm the fever of nationalism he had roused.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alaungpaya” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Alaungpaya</b> besieged Ayutthaya in 1760 but withdrew when he fell ill, dying on the retreat.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya Kingdom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Aung Zeya</b>, the headman of the village of Moksobo north of Ava, refused to swear allegiance to the southern conquerors, rallied the surrounding villages and proclaimed himself king under the name Alaungpaya, ‘future Buddha’.</blockquote><a href="/empire/konbaung-dynasty">Konbaung dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Alaungpaya</b>, the headman of Moksobo, refused to submit, rallied the villages of the dry zone and within a few years drove out the southern forces, founding the Konbaung dynasty that reunited the country on the foundations the Restored Toungoo kings had laid.</blockquote><a href="/empire/restored-toungoo-kingdom">Restored Toungoo Kingdom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Alaungpaya</b> led forty of his best men against a 50-man Hanthawaddy detachment at Halin, south of Shwebo, and wiped it out, in the first resistance of the Konbaung–Hanthawaddy War.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-20">20 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Alaungpaya</b> entered through the Mohnyin Gate, the walls were razed, and the fall ended the Konbaung–Hanthawaddy War.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-6">6 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Alaungpaya</b>, a village headman of Moksobo, raised an army in Upper Burma, retook Ava at the start of 1754 and swept south, capturing Syriam and finally Pegu in 1757.</blockquote><a href="/state/restored-hanthawaddy-kingdom">Restored Hanthawaddy Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shwebo">Shwebo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. Died in Mottama, in what is now <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=22.57,95.7&amp;year=1743" aria-label="Shwebo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7111 1331 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Alaungpaya"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7561 1601h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7561 1601h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=22.57,95.7&amp;year=1743">What was here in 1743? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
